# kk: use stand-in textures for null image and texel buffer descriptors
# https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/46527 state=opened
# ### What does this MR do and why?

<!--Describe in detail what your merge request does and why.-->

```
kk: use stand-in textures for null image and texel buffer descriptors

With nullDescriptor enabled, kk_nir_lower_null_images wraps every image and texel buffer access in an if (handle != 0) branch. DXVK and vkd3d-proton always enable nullDescriptor, so every texture read in those applications pays for the branch.

This MR creates one 1x1 stand-in texture per texture type and component class at device creation (only when nullDescriptor is enabled). Their resource IDs are stored in a small buffer whose address is in the root descriptor table. Reads and queries (image load, size, samples, levels and non-sparse tex) now select the stand-in's ID when the handle is null and select zero for the result, so no branch is needed. Stores, atomics, sparse operations and fences keep the branch. Kinds without a stand-in (multisampled depth arrays, writable cube and multisampled images, other component types) also keep the branch.

The first commit fixes an existing issue: the image fence emitted after a store or atomic was not covered by the null check, so it executed on a null descriptor. A fence directly after the null check around a store or atomic on the same image is now moved into that check.

MESA_KK_DEBUG=force_null_descriptor lowers as if nullDescriptor were enabled, for testing the path with every test.

Testing (Apple M5 Pro, macOS 27.2, CTS 83573e36d58): dEQP-VK.robustness.* (96,147 cases) with force_null_descriptor: no new failures compared to main. A 79,312-case regression list: no new failures. Under MTL_DEBUG_LAYER and MTL_SHADER_VALIDATION, no case is worse than main.

Not covered by CTS: null descriptors of depth images and combined image samplers.
